  {
   "title": "Interpretation (LLM reasoning)",
   "text": "The QSAR model outperforms both the naive mean-based baseline and the nearest-neighbor baseline on held-out test data, with calibrated uncertainty intervals showing empirical coverage close to the nominal target, suggesting the model captures a genuine structure-activity signal rather than memorizing training examples. That said, the correlation between predicted and actual potency on the test set is modest, the training set is fairly small, and the active compounds span a limited number of distinct scaffolds, which together limit how far the model's predictions should be trusted to generalize beyond closely related chemical series. The identity of the target protein and the underlying bioactivity dataset were confirmed consistent prior to modeling, and all reported claims were internally cross-checked with no mismatches found. These results support using the model as a preliminary, in-silico ranking tool for prioritizing further testing within the same chemical space it was trained on \u2014 they do not establish that any specific compound is a viable drug candidate, nor do they speak to potency outside the assay conditions used, selectivity, toxicity, or any other property relevant to therapeutic development.\n\n*This paragraph is LLM reasoning over the findings above. It is tagged HYPOTHESIS: it contains no numbers of its own and was itself checked against the claims ledger before publication.*",
